<figure class="journal-illustration"><img src="/assets/images/navaratri-garba-journal.webp" alt="Illustrated Gujarati garba gathering with dancers circling a lit clay garbo in a courtyard at dusk" width="1536" height="1024" loading="lazy"><figcaption>Garba during Navaratri · AI-generated cultural illustration.</figcaption></figure>
      <p>Navaratri means “nine nights.” Devotion to the Goddess finds expression through worship, stories, music and community gatherings. Different regions give the celebration distinct forms.</p>
      <h4>A tradition with many local voices</h4>
      <dl class="journal-facts"><div><dt>Gujarat</dt><dd>Garba brings people together in circular dance and devotional celebration.</dd></div><div><dt>West Bengal</dt><dd>Durga Puja centres on the Goddess through elaborate images, worship and public celebration.</dd></div><div><dt>Tamil Nadu</dt><dd>Golu displays arrange dolls and figurines as part of the festival’s storytelling and devotional world.</dd></div></dl>
      <p>These examples introduce a wider diversity; they do not describe every household or community. The Devi and Mahishasura narrative offers one doorway into the festival’s theme of divine protection.</p>
      <p class="editorial-reflection"><strong>A question to carry:</strong> What can a story, a dance and a carefully arranged display each help us remember?</p>
